WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:15.100
ANDERSON: Good afternoon.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:16.100
I'm Lisa Anderson.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:22.700
And I'm delighted to be able to welcome David
Miliband and Peter Wittig to be with us today.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:29.100
As you know, this is the 12th Annual Arthur
C. Helton Memorial Lecture.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:35.680
And as many of you will remember, Arthur was
director of peace and conflict studies here

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:42.010
at the Council, and senior fellow for refugee
studies and preventive action.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:48.890
He was killed in 2003 when the hotel in Baghdad
when he and 20 other international civil servants

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:56.149
were serving in the mission of the U.N. secretary-general's
special representative for Iraq-when that

00:00:56.149 --> 00:00:57.520
hotel was bombed.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:03.499
This lecture is dedicated to his lifetime's
mission, serving the world's humanitarian

00:01:03.499 --> 00:01:05.800
and refugee crises.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.030
And I'm particularly pleased to be able to
welcome Jackie Gilbert, his widow, and Pamela

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:16.980
Krause, his sister, and all of Arthur's friends
to be with us here today.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:23.230
As you know, we are joined by David Miliband
in his capacity as president and chief executive

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:28.860
officer of the International Rescue Committee,
formerly secretary of state for foreign affairs

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.380
of the U.K., as you know.

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:35.090
And Peter Wittig is ambassador of the Federal
Republic of Germany to the United States,

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:38.780
also served here at the United Nations.

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:40.620
Thank you both for being with us.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.820
Yesterday, as most of you know, was World
Refugee Day.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:48.390
And it came with more dismaying news from
the UNHCR.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:52.750
Wars and persecution have driven more people
from their homes than at any time since the

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:56.030
United Nations began keeping records.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:02:04.340
On average, 24 people were forced to flee
each minute in 2015-four times more than a

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.700
decade ago.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:12.560
Now we have about 65 million people displaced.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:19.061
And it's the first time since the U.N. was
established that we've crossed the 60 million

00:02:19.061 --> 00:02:20.470
mark.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:28.450
Twenty-five million of those have taken refuge
outside their home country.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:33.629
The dangers are at home, obviously, it's why
people are moving, in transit, and in the

00:02:33.629 --> 00:02:35.260
receiving countries.

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.790
As the U.N. high commissioner said recently,
more people are being displaced by war and

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:45.400
persecution, and that's worrying in itself,
but the factors that endanger refugees are

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:46.620
multiplying too.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.450
At sea, a frightening number of refugees and
migrants are dying each year.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:58.159
On land, people are fleeing war, finding their
way to blocked and closed borders.

00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:01.530
Closing borders, he said, does not solve the
problem.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:06.180
I'll be turning to Ambassador Wittig to give
us a sense of some of the political ramifications

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.999
of this exodus, particularly in Europe and
North America.

00:03:09.999 --> 00:03:12.719
But perhaps we could start with you, David.

00:03:12.719 --> 00:03:17.810
Can you tell us a little bit about who the
refugees in the Middle East are, where are

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:25.459
they, what are they fleeing, why are we seeing
this kind of enormous uptick in this problem?

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:31.090
MILIBAND: Well, thank you very much for the
question and thank you to all of you for being

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.090
out here.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:36.400
I should first of all knowledge that it's
a real honor to be commemorating the extraordinary

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:42.840
life of Arthur Helton, who was clearly a humanitarian
of extraordinary dedication and devotion.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:47.940
And so I really feel privileged to be here
today.

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.530
You asked about the Middle East, but I think
it's right to put the figures that you gave

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.390
in a global context.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:04:00.320
Twenty-one million refugees, 40 million internally
displaced, 3 million asylees in the 65 million

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.579
figure.

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:08.499
And while Syria and the Syria crisis is the
poster child for the worst of the humanitarian

00:04:08.499 --> 00:04:16.980
crisis, it's important to remember that Somalia's
civil war carries on two generations on, Congo,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.890
massive refugee flows.

00:04:18.890 --> 00:04:24.780
I was in the northeast of Nigeria at the end
of last year, 2.2 million internally displaced

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:25.980
people there.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:31.810
So of the 21 million refugees, 5 million are
from Syria.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:33.940
Seven million IDPs, internally displaced.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.639
Over 40 million, 7 million are Syrian.

00:04:36.639 --> 00:04:42.500
Specifically on your question, where are the
Syrian refugees?

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:51.030
They're predominantly in the region, about
4 1/4 million are in the Middle East region.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:56.080
And that is consistent with the global trend,
which is that-contrary to the image that you

00:04:56.080 --> 00:05:02.800
believe if you read the newspaper-there are
not hordes of refugees entering Western societies.

00:05:02.800 --> 00:05:06.889
The vast bulk of refugees, about 85 percent,
are in poor countries, not rich countries-poor

00:05:06.889 --> 00:05:10.870
countries are joining countries in conflict.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:16.840
Of the 5-plus million Syrian refugees, Turkey
has the largest number, 2.5 to 2.7 million

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:19.860
refugees, which is an extraordinary load.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:29.300
Lebanon, a country of only 4 1/2 to 5 million
people, you know Lebanon well, has at least

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.169
1.5 million refugees.

00:05:32.169 --> 00:05:38.599
So fully one-third of the Lebanese population
now are Syrian refugees.

00:05:38.599 --> 00:05:43.879
Jordan, there's some contest over the figures-650,000
official figures.

00:05:43.879 --> 00:05:47.009
The government of Jordan would say there are
more.

00:05:47.009 --> 00:05:52.930
There's about 250,000 Syrians in Iraq, actually,
who in our experience, we've got 2,000 people

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:57.729
on the ground inside of Syria and about 1,500
in the neighboring states.

00:05:57.729 --> 00:06:03.199
The 250,000 Syrian refugees in Iraq probably
get better help, more help than the internally

00:06:03.199 --> 00:06:07.569
displaced from Mosul and elsewhere Iraq.

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:09.870
And then you have the European end of the
crisis.

00:06:09.870 --> 00:06:16.060
A million or so asylum seekers arrived in
Europe last year, not all of them, though,

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:17.479
from the Middle East.

00:06:17.479 --> 00:06:22.130
I mean, probably a third of them came from-or
even slightly more, maybe 40 percent-came

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:25.750
from Afghanistan, maybe Somalia, maybe elsewhere.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:30.560
And certainly the bulk inside Europe at the
moment are that million.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:31.759
There are 50,000 in Greece.

00:06:31.759 --> 00:06:35.460
The vast bulk of them, probably 900,000-around
900,000 are in Germany.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:42.449
Sweden has a smaller number-much smaller number,
in the tens of thousands.

00:06:42.449 --> 00:06:51.570
And the final part of the jigsaw is that while
the flow from Syria into Europe has been more

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:57.639
or less paused by the quote, unquote "deal"
between the European Union and Turkey, demonstrating

00:06:57.639 --> 00:07:01.770
more about the power of the Turkish government
to turn on the tap and turn off the tap than

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:09.139
what it demonstrates about the deal, the tragic
scenes that were in the news two weeks ago

00:07:09.139 --> 00:07:11.819
were the second route to Europe.

00:07:11.819 --> 00:07:14.000
The first route being from Turkey to Greece.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.719
The second route being from North Africa to
Italy.

00:07:17.719 --> 00:07:25.889
And in our judgment-we've got people in Libya
as well as in Greece and in Serbia and Turkey-is

00:07:25.889 --> 00:07:31.949
that you've not yet seen displacement of Syrian
refugees from Turkey round into North Africa,

00:07:31.949 --> 00:07:35.580
and then going north into Europe-or trying
to go north into Europe.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:40.250
You've got essentially Africans going through
North Africa-or going through African and

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:43.189
then up into North Africa, and then trying
to get to Italy.

00:07:43.189 --> 00:07:48.550
One in 20, two weeks ago, were dying on the
way in the sea.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:49.659
So you can see the gravity.

00:07:49.659 --> 00:07:53.969
Sorry, that's probably a longer answer than
you wanted, but-

00:07:53.969 --> 00:08:01.030
ANDERSON: So, it's actually what I did want,
that is to say the scale of this problem is-it

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:08.110
may not be precisely focused on Europe as
much as we, and in Europe, think is the case,

00:08:08.110 --> 00:08:09.870
but it's a staggering problem.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:14.810
And it will be a problem-I wonder, before
we get to the receiving countries a little

00:08:14.810 --> 00:08:22.699
bit, whether there are changes in, if you
will, the patterns of who is displaced and

00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:28.030
who is-because it seems to me the press reports,
you see a lot of families, you see a lot of

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:29.120
children.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:31.720
Is that typical of refugee flows?

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:37.590
It seems to me that one wouldn't expect to
see as much of the, you know, unaccompanied

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:41.320
children and families with very small children
and so forth.

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:44.660
MILIBAND: I mean, it's very mixed.

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:48.320
And different stages of the Syrian crisis
have seen different people leave.

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:55.960
I mean, to say the obvious, the wealthiest
people left first, right at the beginning.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:01.519
You then had a period in 2011 where people
were hoping it would be a short war and so

00:09:01.519 --> 00:09:05.130
the refugee flows were relatively limited.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:10.501
But then by 2012, as the scale of the-the
intensity of the conflict grew, you had a

00:09:10.501 --> 00:09:13.720
large number of people getting out of Syria.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:17.339
And I think there's no reason to believe it
wouldn't be families trying to get out because

00:09:17.339 --> 00:09:22.010
men take their wife and kids as well as widowed
women-

00:09:22.010 --> 00:09:23.010
ANDERSON: Right.

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:25.040
MILIBAND: -with kids getting out.

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:28.590
But then when it came to the flow to Europe,
there's been an ebb and flow in the numbers.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:32.860
So there were periods when there would be
large numbers of men going and then swinging

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:37.610
back to a much more balanced grouping.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:41.040
And you've seen-I don't know how many of you
have seen the pictures of people crossing

00:09:41.040 --> 00:09:46.230
from Turkey into Greece, but lots of kids,
lots of women on those-

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:47.230
ANDERSON: Exactly.

00:09:47.230 --> 00:09:48.230
MILIBAND: -on those shores.

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:51.970
Maybe the ambassador can comment on this,
but I think in Northern Europe-it's said that

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:58.650
in Sweden and in Denmark there's a lot more
men who are applying for asylum in Northern

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.770
Europe, in Scandinavia.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:04.040
But essentially you've got people fleeing
for their lives, and people go with their

00:10:04.040 --> 00:10:08.149
families if they can, if at all possible to
do so.

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:13.990
It's worth saying just-I'm sure we'll come
to this later-the U.S. has a flagrantly low

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:17.731
number of Syrians who have come here, two-and-a-half
thousand, but one interesting point is that

00:10:17.731 --> 00:10:21.100
less than 2 percent of the two-and-a-half
thousand Syrians who have come here in the

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.240
last five years are single men.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:27.029
So one of the myths put round is that there's
loads of single men-

00:10:27.029 --> 00:10:28.029
ANDERSON: Right.

00:10:28.029 --> 00:10:29.029
MILIBAND: -coming into America, but it's actually
not true.

00:10:29.029 --> 00:10:30.060
ANDERSON: Right.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:31.060
OK.

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:35.360
I think that, in fact, answers the question
exactly.

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:38.339
So the humanitarian issues would seem completely
compelling.

00:10:38.339 --> 00:10:48.200
We see the pictures, we see what's happening
and so forth, and yet in Europe-and in Germany,

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:52.660
in the EU as a whole, even in North America
there is enormous resistance.

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:55.810
Where is the resistance coming from?

00:10:55.810 --> 00:11:00.350
Talk a little bit about how this is seen,
particularly from Germany and Europe.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:05.490
WITTIG: Well, let me first of all thank you,
Lisa and Richard and the Council, for having

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:06.490
me here.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:12.550
I'm also privileged to be on a panel with
David Miliband, whose work and whose organization

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:16.800
I really admire for the wonderful work they're
doing worldwide.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:20.160
You said a record number of people are uprooted.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:27.709
I think we've got to understand this is a
movement of people of epic proportions.

00:11:27.709 --> 00:11:33.500
It is the first digital migration of people.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:39.020
And I think we've got to realize it will not
go away in a year or two or three.

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:42.580
This is with us to stay for a long time.

00:11:42.580 --> 00:11:46.120
And this is something we've got to communicate
to our people.

00:11:46.120 --> 00:11:50.730
And I'm speaking here on behalf of my government.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:54.070
And that is a difficult message to convey.

00:11:54.070 --> 00:12:01.089
The message is there's no single lever to
pull to stop this flow of refugees, of asylum

00:12:01.089 --> 00:12:07.769
seekers, of migrants, but we have to address
the complexity of the situation through sort

00:12:07.769 --> 00:12:10.110
of multilayered measures.

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:12.079
We have to look at the root causes.

00:12:12.079 --> 00:12:14.649
We have to address the root causes of conflict.

00:12:14.649 --> 00:12:17.950
That means we are in there for the long haul.

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:24.790
We've got to support the neighboring countries
of those that are adjacent to the zones of

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:31.089
conflict-in the case of Syria it's Lebanon,
it's Jordan, it's Turkey-so that the refugees

00:12:31.089 --> 00:12:37.400
preferably stay there so in order to return
easily once the conflict is over.

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:43.210
And in the case of Europe, we have to come
to a common European refugee and asylum policy,

00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:50.100
which is a tall order in a European Union
that consists of 28 sovereign nation states.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:58.420
And then on a national basis, we have to think
hard and engage proactively in an integration

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:00.120
policy.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:06.519
Of course we hope that many refugees can return
home because the conflict has been solved

00:13:06.519 --> 00:13:10.350
or the cause of persecution, but we've got
to be realistic.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:18.779
A lot of them that come to our countries will
stay, and we've got to begin now to make-go

00:13:18.779 --> 00:13:22.160
the extra mile, make extra efforts to integrate
them.

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:29.530
So no easy answers, no one lever to pull,
no one wall, no shutting off borders, but

00:13:29.530 --> 00:13:36.740
addressing the complexity of the issue, and
that is difficult to digest for, you know,

00:13:36.740 --> 00:13:39.030
the rank and file voters in many countries.

00:13:39.030 --> 00:13:45.320
And it has had huge repercussions not only
domestically in many countries of Europe-it

00:13:45.320 --> 00:13:52.660
has upended in some cases the political-the
party system-but also it has put a lot of

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:57.209
strain on the coherence of the European Union.

00:13:57.209 --> 00:14:06.800
And it is probably the most challenging-I
hesitate to say existential, but almost existential

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:09.449
crisis of Europe since World War II.

00:14:09.449 --> 00:14:16.459
So this is the effect it had, compounded by
other factors, on the European Union.

00:14:16.459 --> 00:14:20.959
ANDERSON: I actually-I'd address this to either
one of you, actually.

00:14:20.959 --> 00:14:26.660
One of the things that's sort of a puzzle
is that we treat refugees as a crisis and

00:14:26.660 --> 00:14:34.509
an emergency, but as you suggest, this is
sort of a permanent-or we should be addressing

00:14:34.509 --> 00:14:40.230
this as if it were a permanent element of
modern life.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:45.889
And as I understand it, most people are refugees
for a very long time, even those people who

00:14:45.889 --> 00:14:49.279
do ultimately return home.

00:14:49.279 --> 00:14:52.260
So how do you deal with a permanent crisis?

00:14:52.260 --> 00:14:58.709
MILIBAND: Well, we are an international refugee
resettlement agency in the U.S. but we're

00:14:58.709 --> 00:15:02.940
also an international humanitarian aid agency,
so we see the full arc of crisis.

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:07.810
And I think-first of all, a factual point:
Less than 1 percent of the world's refugees

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:08.889
went home last year.

00:15:08.889 --> 00:15:09.889
ANDERSON: Right.

00:15:09.889 --> 00:15:12.380
MILIBAND: So people aren't going home.

00:15:12.380 --> 00:15:17.449
If you go to the Dadaab refugee camp, which
is the world's largest refugee camp, in eastern

00:15:17.449 --> 00:15:20.481
Kenya, 360,000 people live there.

00:15:20.481 --> 00:15:21.481
It's a small city.

00:15:21.481 --> 00:15:23.660
A hundred-thousand people were born there.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:25.480
So if you say, will you ever go home?

00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:28.110
They say, well, what do you mean; this is
my home.

00:15:28.110 --> 00:15:31.150
So I think that's just the information point.

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:36.460
I think that-my own view is that this, quote,
unquote, "crisis," if you say, how are we

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:40.680
going to deal with this state of permanent
crisis, it suggests a degree of stability,

00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.250
whereas this is not a stable crisis.

00:15:43.250 --> 00:15:48.339
It's a fast-it's a dynamic crisis because
it's not-it would be bad enough if you had

00:15:48.339 --> 00:15:52.550
20 million refugees year after year after
year.

00:15:52.550 --> 00:15:53.550
That's not the case.

00:15:53.550 --> 00:15:54.649
You've got growing numbers of refugees.

00:15:54.649 --> 00:15:59.829
I mean, the world's newest nation, South Sudan,
has added 150,000 refugees in the last two

00:15:59.829 --> 00:16:00.829
years.

00:16:00.829 --> 00:16:07.940
So you've got this-these trends, the weak
states that are unable to meet basic needs

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:12.110
and are unable to contain difference within
peaceful boundaries.

00:16:12.110 --> 00:16:17.750
You've got a weak international system which
is divided at its core.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:22.740
And the ambassador can speak to some of the
elements of that from both his New York experience

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:25.350
and his current position.

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:32.209
And you've got, sorry to say it, fundamental
questions being asked in the Islamic world

00:16:32.209 --> 00:16:36.360
about the reconciliation of Islam with modernity.

00:16:36.360 --> 00:16:43.380
And it is-I always-I feel honor-bound to say
to audiences, the IRC was founded by Einstein

00:16:43.380 --> 00:16:44.990
to rescue Jews from Europe.

00:16:44.990 --> 00:16:51.519
Today, 40 (percent) to 50 percent of our work
is in Muslim-majority countries where religion

00:16:51.519 --> 00:16:56.300
is at the core of some genuinely existential
questions, and the Syria crisis is very good

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:57.300
example.

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:02.019
It's not the case that all Muslim majority
countries are unable to reconcile Islam with

00:17:02.019 --> 00:17:07.480
modernity-you know, Indonesia, Bangladesh,
et cetera-but if I think about some of the

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:11.940
biggest programs we run in Syria, in Afghanistan,
even in the Central African Republic, where

00:17:11.940 --> 00:17:18.350
it's actually religious tension between Muslims
and Christians, you've got really deep, long-term

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:20.750
issues that are not going to be resolved anytime
soon.

00:17:20.750 --> 00:17:28.209
So I think that the roots of the crisis are
important to diagnose if we're to even begin

00:17:28.209 --> 00:17:29.990
to think about what are the antidotes.

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:36.289
ANDERSON: Well, it is true; apparently two-thirds
of all refugees are Muslims, suggesting that

00:17:36.289 --> 00:17:42.559
there is some issues there in the Muslim world
that have to be addressed because they're

00:17:42.559 --> 00:17:44.720
just sending people out.

00:17:44.720 --> 00:17:47.880
They're not, obviously, happy or comfortable
at home.

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:56.200
But that also suggests ways of thinking about
why it is that people are so concerned about

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:59.970
absorbing these populations, particularly
in Europe.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:09.530
WITTIG: Well, I think that that is one elephant
in the room, that most of the refugees that

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:13.880
are coming now to Europe are of Muslim faith.

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:19.590
And I still think, at least in my country,
there's general support for the refugee policy

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:23.220
of Chancellor Merkel.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:33.730
But there is a growing doubt whether the values
of some of those refugees or migrants or asylum-seekers

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:39.659
are compatible with the values of our liberal
democracy.

00:18:39.659 --> 00:18:51.030
And that's why we have now a right-wing party
that puts Islam on top of the agenda, saying

00:18:51.030 --> 00:18:53.990
they have to adapt to our values.

00:18:53.990 --> 00:19:01.340
So it is-it goes deep into the psychology
of people, of voters in Europe, much deeper

00:19:01.340 --> 00:19:09.870
than in the U.S. Ironically, only-you know,
the U.S. has only 0.6 (percent) Muslims as

00:19:09.870 --> 00:19:10.940
its population.

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:12.150
In Germany, it's 5 percent.

00:19:12.150 --> 00:19:14.010
In France, it's 7 or 8 percent.

00:19:14.010 --> 00:19:17.010
So the Muslim population is bigger.

00:19:17.010 --> 00:19:21.990
But it plays a very big role in the discussion.

00:19:21.990 --> 00:19:29.710
What we do now in Germany is we offer not
only language courses or programs to get people

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:36.289
into jobs more easily or assisted-give them
assisted vocational training, we also offer

00:19:36.289 --> 00:19:45.100
so-called integration courses teaching the
newcomers about the values of our democracy,

00:19:45.100 --> 00:19:54.940
including respect for gender, for other religions;
in our case, you know, respecting our laws

00:19:54.940 --> 00:20:01.330
concerning the Holocaust, denial of Holocaust,
our special relationship to Israel, et cetera.

00:20:01.330 --> 00:20:12.669
So we have to proactively do something to
show that Islam and our values of the liberal

00:20:12.669 --> 00:20:15.330
democracy can be compatible, but that's a
challenge.

00:20:15.330 --> 00:20:18.071
MILIBAND: Although it's interesting that,
remember, two-thirds of the American public

00:20:18.071 --> 00:20:19.870
didn't want Jews to come here in the 1930s.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:20.870
ANDERSON: Right.

00:20:20.870 --> 00:20:23.270
MILIBAND: So the figures are similar generation
to generation.

00:20:23.270 --> 00:20:27.710
Now, in the U.K.-I did promise Richard I'd
get Brexit in somehow into this-(laughter)-in

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:35.840
the U.K., a lot of-the whole of the debate
about migration is about Catholics from Poland

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:36.911
rather than about Muslims.

00:20:36.911 --> 00:20:45.059
So it's important-I mean, the truth is that
how different-how people who are different

00:20:45.059 --> 00:20:48.309
from each other learn to live together is
one of the defining issues of the modern age.

00:20:48.309 --> 00:20:49.309
(Chuckles.)

00:20:49.309 --> 00:20:50.309
I mean, that's the-that's the point.

00:20:50.309 --> 00:20:51.309
ANDERSON: Right, right.

00:20:51.309 --> 00:20:55.930
You know, and of course, when you think about
Germany, and you've-Germany has had Turkish

00:20:55.930 --> 00:21:02.730
workers for decades, and of course France
has had people from North Africa for decades.

00:21:02.730 --> 00:21:08.750
So there is something that this is sparking
an anxiety that goes well beyond the kind

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:13.700
of modern history of-
WITTIG: Well, there were some lessons from

00:21:13.700 --> 00:21:17.770
the Turkish immigration in the '50s and '60s
to be drawn.

00:21:17.770 --> 00:21:26.620
I mean, there was immigration from a moderate,
very moderate Islamic nation, you know, still

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:31.790
under the banner of the secular Atat�rk
philosophy.

00:21:31.790 --> 00:21:36.730
Today, it's a different context.

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:44.470
But I think the lesson that we draw here is
that we have to proactively offer language,

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:53.730
offer job opportunities, offer vocational
training, and offer our sort of vision of

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:57.460
a liberal democratic order.

00:21:57.460 --> 00:22:04.600
And we-I think in the-in the long run we would
need a kind of European Islam, you know, that

00:22:04.600 --> 00:22:07.919
is compatible with-and I think it is possible.

00:22:07.919 --> 00:22:15.800
And, you know, London elected a Muslim mayor,
perfectly in sync with all the values of the

00:22:15.800 --> 00:22:16.800
U.K.

00:22:16.800 --> 00:22:23.220
So it is-it is possible, but it takes, I think,
active measures to accomplish that.

00:22:23.220 --> 00:22:28.600
ANDERSON: Let me ask one final question of
the two of you before opening it to the members

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.299
here.

00:22:30.299 --> 00:22:32.590
You in a sense represent different approaches.

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:33.779
One is governmental.

00:22:33.779 --> 00:22:40.010
I mean, clearly the public sector-the government
of Germany, of the EU, the United Nations

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:43.730
all have a sort of, you know, obvious role.

00:22:43.730 --> 00:22:48.170
You represent the civil society kind of perspective.

00:22:48.170 --> 00:22:57.419
Are there ways that you think that either-that
you imagine more effective collaboration across

00:22:57.419 --> 00:22:58.419
sectors?

00:22:58.419 --> 00:23:02.779
Is there a role the private sector might play
here?

00:23:02.779 --> 00:23:10.909
Are we missing creative opportunities to think
about addressing this permanent emergency?

00:23:10.909 --> 00:23:12.409
(Laughter.)

00:23:12.409 --> 00:23:17.789
MILIBAND: It's the fault of the politicians,
so why don't you go first and then I'll explain

00:23:17.789 --> 00:23:19.049
how we're cleaning up.

00:23:19.049 --> 00:23:20.049
(Laughter.)

00:23:20.049 --> 00:23:27.350
WITTIG: I think the magnitude of this-of this
inflow of refugees and asylum-seekers and

00:23:27.350 --> 00:23:31.200
migrants cannot be tackled by governments
alone.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:40.910
It is a sort of a multi-stakeholder affair,
if you will, and we need all the volunteers.

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:50.760
And in my country, to my great pleasant surprise,
we've seen, you know, an outburst of volunteerism

00:23:50.760 --> 00:23:53.320
when the refugees came, and in great numbers.

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:56.600
So we need that civil society engagement.

00:23:56.600 --> 00:23:58.850
And we need, of course, the business to go
along.

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:01.640
And this is where the jobs have to come from.

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:08.190
And some of the even big businesses in Germany
had a sort of avant-garde function.

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:13.669
You know, I don't want to name brands, but
the big automakers immediately offered, you

00:24:13.669 --> 00:24:16.940
know, places for apprenticeships and so on.

00:24:16.940 --> 00:24:21.460
So this is not just a thing of the governments.

00:24:21.460 --> 00:24:27.960
It is the whole society, including business
and the civil society has to be brought into

00:24:27.960 --> 00:24:29.610
that-into that challenge.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:34.210
MILIBAND: Well, I strongly agree with that,
but I'd frame it as follows.

00:24:34.210 --> 00:24:40.740
The humanitarian sector has been an afterthought
in politics, government, and diplomacy for

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:41.740
a very long time.

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:46.770
It's been an act of charity that has been
reactive, that has been defined by natural

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:54.970
disasters and emergencies, that has been expected
to be short term, and that has been anticipated

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:58.270
to be succeeded by a return to the status
quo ante.

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:00.200
None of those things are true.

00:25:00.200 --> 00:25:06.779
And if we carry on trying to run an old-style
charitable, reactive, short-term system, we're

00:25:06.779 --> 00:25:09.429
going to continue to fail, which is what we're
doing at the moment.

00:25:09.429 --> 00:25:13.960
And the gap between humanitarian need and
humanitarian provision is going to grow, which

00:25:13.960 --> 00:25:15.871
it's doing at the moment.

00:25:15.871 --> 00:25:18.270
It's not just there are more people in need
compared to the growth in the number of people

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:23.910
who are being helped, it's that the complexity
and depth of their needs is outstripping the

00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:26.720
capacity of the sector to respond.

00:25:26.720 --> 00:25:31.159
And I think it's very, very important that
we think in completely new ways about the

00:25:31.159 --> 00:25:32.610
way this sector runs.

00:25:32.610 --> 00:25:36.789
The sector is defined by refugee camps; but
60 percent of refugees are not in camps, they're

00:25:36.789 --> 00:25:37.789
in urban areas.

00:25:37.789 --> 00:25:41.809
So they need employment and they need housing
a wholly different method than before.

00:25:41.809 --> 00:25:46.250
The sector is defined by short-term lifesaving,
when in fact for the generations that are

00:25:46.250 --> 00:25:50.810
in camps or in urban areas, education is as
important as health care.

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:54.940
That's not been taken on board in any systematic
way at all.

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:58.070
Thirdly, refugees are in countries that aren't
presumed to be poor.

00:25:58.070 --> 00:26:03.490
And the whole of the development system-the
system of tackling poverty globally-is based

00:26:03.490 --> 00:26:08.731
on the fiction that you find poor people in
poor countries, whereas actually there are

00:26:08.731 --> 00:26:15.630
lots of poor people in fragile, lower-middle-income
countries like Lebanon, Jordan, Kenya, Ethiopia.

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:20.029
And so we need to change the way in which
the global system thinks about poverty and

00:26:20.029 --> 00:26:21.650
crisis in a very serious way.

00:26:21.650 --> 00:26:29.169
And I would put on your agenda, for the Council,
there's a U.N. secretary-general being appointed.

00:26:29.169 --> 00:26:36.210
What questions are being asked about the vision,
ideas, commitments of that person to address

00:26:36.210 --> 00:26:37.210
these points?

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:41.760
We're actually going to release in the next
week or so eight or nine questions that we're

00:26:41.760 --> 00:26:47.200
going to send to all of the candidates, asking
for their response on how they're going to

00:26:47.200 --> 00:26:51.160
prioritize and address the humanitarian crisis.

00:26:51.160 --> 00:26:55.630
Because in the public-it's very good that
there have been public fora, but very few

00:26:55.630 --> 00:26:58.799
questions have been about the humanitarian
system.

00:26:58.799 --> 00:27:05.429
And the humanitarian-the questions of humanitarian
policy go to the heart of THE fundamental

00:27:05.429 --> 00:27:10.190
question for the next secretary-general, which
is, is he or she more of a secretary or more

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:11.529
of a general?

00:27:11.529 --> 00:27:12.529
(Laughter.)

00:27:12.529 --> 00:27:17.090
And I say that in all seriousness, because
either they are there to take the notes of

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:24.890
the big powers or they're there to actually
be a more active and proactive participant

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:26.399
in the shaping of the decisions of the big
powers.

00:27:26.399 --> 00:27:32.200
And it's obvious that the secretary-general
of the U.N. serves at the-with the confidence

00:27:32.200 --> 00:27:35.280
not just of the Security Council, but of the
whole General Assembly.

00:27:35.280 --> 00:27:39.789
But it's also important that this person is
empowered to make a difference, not just to

00:27:39.789 --> 00:27:43.330
maintain the status quo, because the status
quo is so obviously failing.

00:27:43.330 --> 00:27:50.419
ANDERSON: Well, this-we could discuss the
implications of sort of normalizing the responsibility

00:27:50.419 --> 00:27:55.940
to protect, and de-militarizing the responsibility
to protect and so forth might be one way of

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:57.460
thinking about that.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:28:01.919
But let me now open the floor for comments.

00:28:01.919 --> 00:28:08.049
Now, let me remind you, before you say anything,
that this meeting is on the record.

00:28:08.049 --> 00:28:09.049
Wait for the microphone.

00:28:09.049 --> 00:28:10.110
We'll start right over here.

00:28:10.110 --> 00:28:16.019
And please tell us-stand, tell us who you
are-sir, right here, yes.

00:28:16.019 --> 00:28:20.500
Tell us who you are, your name and affiliation,
and then be concise.

00:28:20.500 --> 00:28:24.210
Q: Bill Drozdiak, McLarty Associates.

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:32.360
I wanted to ask both speakers, how do you
deal with the policy conflict between two

00:28:32.360 --> 00:28:33.360
factors.

00:28:33.360 --> 00:28:35.160
One is the demographics.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:41.679
In Europe, Peter, everybody knows for decades
now there's been an enormous shortfall.

00:28:41.679 --> 00:28:47.640
So one would think politicians should be able
to persuade their people of the need to embraced

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:55.630
more immigrants in order to sustain their
lifestyle and pay for pensions in the future.

00:28:55.630 --> 00:29:03.160
And the same, David, in Britain, where you've
had 330,000 come from this year, even though

00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:06.710
the government said there would only be 100,000.

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:10.530
And it's been a benefit for the U.K. economy.

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:16.140
If it is politically unpalatable, to what
extent would people and politicians be willing

00:29:16.140 --> 00:29:26.970
to pay for massive Marshall Plan-like programs
to develop economic programs in North Africa

00:29:26.970 --> 00:29:32.159
and the Middle East in order to encourage
these young people to stay home, particularly

00:29:32.159 --> 00:29:37.320
given the fact that 70 percent of their populations
are under 30?

00:29:37.320 --> 00:29:40.539
MILIBAND: Great question.

00:29:40.539 --> 00:29:44.970
I think that the-and I'm obviously based in
New York now, so I'm speaking about the U.K.

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:48.890
from a distance, but to fair to the current
government they've maintained what the previous

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:52.570
government started, which was Marshall Plan-style
levels of expenditure on overseas aid.

00:29:52.570 --> 00:29:55.320
The 0.7 percent of GDP is spent on overseas
aid.

00:29:55.320 --> 00:29:56.539
It's very, very good.

00:29:56.539 --> 00:30:01.230
The U.S. figure is 0.21 percent, just by way
of comparison.

00:30:01.230 --> 00:30:08.080
Now, the difficulty, I think, is less about
the funding, which if you've got determined

00:30:08.080 --> 00:30:10.070
political leadership you can get away with.

00:30:10.070 --> 00:30:12.870
I think that the more fundamental question
is twofold.

00:30:12.870 --> 00:30:17.940
One, is it right to decide on refugee intake
on the basis on demographics?

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:19.690
I would say no.

00:30:19.690 --> 00:30:25.049
The point about the 1951 Refugee Convention
is it gave rights-absolute rights to refugees

00:30:25.049 --> 00:30:27.720
who have got a well-founded fear of persecution.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:31.179
And my own experience is that when the debate
about refugees and the debate about immigration

00:30:31.179 --> 00:30:33.490
get confused, you end up in trouble.

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:38.289
And immigration policy can be decided on the
basis of meeting demographic and other economic

00:30:38.289 --> 00:30:39.289
needs.

00:30:39.289 --> 00:30:45.230
But a refugee policy needs to honor the distinction
between a refugee and an immigrant.

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:49.730
And I think it's quite important to pick that
up.

00:30:49.730 --> 00:30:56.110
The second thing is that the speed of integration,
or the scale of the flow and the speed of

00:30:56.110 --> 00:30:57.210
integration are very, very tricky.

00:30:57.210 --> 00:31:01.360
I mean, there's a huge challenge in the ambassador's
country.

00:31:01.360 --> 00:31:06.370
If half of those who are applying for asylum,
for the sake of argument, are deigned to be

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:13.429
refugees, and the rest of Europe doesn't pick
up the relocation slack, that's a massive

00:31:13.429 --> 00:31:20.309
integration challenge in any-however efficient
the state and civil society, that's a massive

00:31:20.309 --> 00:31:22.490
challenge.

00:31:22.490 --> 00:31:27.710
And I think that our experience in the U.S.,
we work in 29 U.S. cities, is that it is possible

00:31:27.710 --> 00:31:31.380
to integrate large numbers of people, as long
as you spread them out.

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:35.740
And I think that that's the conundrum that
the German government faces.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:41.720
WITTIG: Yeah, Will, I agree, demography is
an important aspect.

00:31:41.720 --> 00:31:44.549
We are an aging society in Germany.

00:31:44.549 --> 00:31:46.389
We need immigration.

00:31:46.389 --> 00:31:52.440
And I think that has been a paradigm shift
in the last decades that people realize we

00:31:52.440 --> 00:31:54.529
need and we want immigration.

00:31:54.529 --> 00:31:59.280
But I agree with David, you shouldn't confound
those two things.

00:31:59.280 --> 00:32:01.409
The one is a humanitarian challenge.

00:32:01.409 --> 00:32:09.350
And you cannot expect by taking in a big number
of refugees in a short time that they will

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:11.420
help to change the demography.

00:32:11.420 --> 00:32:15.309
It is a huge investment and it's a huge integration
challenge.

00:32:15.309 --> 00:32:21.450
It will take decades to integrate them successfully.

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:33.820
By the way, you mentioned sort of the tendency
that they cluster all in certain usually urban

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:34.820
areas.

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:45.970
And we are now in a way not only stimulate
them spread out, but even via legislation

00:32:45.970 --> 00:32:53.929
oblige them not to go all to the same big
cities, because we fear that then they will

00:32:53.929 --> 00:33:03.539
sort of form their sub-cities and, you know,
and it makes integration more difficult.

00:33:03.539 --> 00:33:09.269
That's a measure to sort of distribute them
all over the country, which is not uncontroversial.

00:33:09.269 --> 00:33:13.149
Some people oppose that, also from the NGO
community.

00:33:13.149 --> 00:33:21.590
But those are all measures to help coping
with this really momentous integration challenge

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:24.820
that we will all face in Europe.

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:31.340
Some countries-and I again come back to Europe
and to the fault lines that have emerged in

00:33:31.340 --> 00:33:33.270
Europe over the refugee crisis.

00:33:33.270 --> 00:33:40.130
Some Eastern European countries coming from
a different background.

00:33:40.130 --> 00:33:43.860
They are not-they are still very homogeneous
societies.

00:33:43.860 --> 00:33:47.110
They didn't have any immigration tradition.

00:33:47.110 --> 00:33:51.840
So they find it difficult to share the burden,
take in some immigrants.

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:58.380
But in the medium and long term, we have to
come to a European burden sharing.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:34:06.730
And I think those countries also have to go
through a steep learning curve that immigration

00:34:06.730 --> 00:34:13.290
is, for all of our countries, an essential,
and in the decades to come.

00:34:13.290 --> 00:34:15.190
ANDERSON: Thank you.

00:34:15.190 --> 00:34:16.190
Right here.

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:17.190
Thank you.

00:34:17.190 --> 00:34:18.200
Q: Thank you.

00:34:18.200 --> 00:34:21.460
Joanna Weschler with Security Council Report.

00:34:21.460 --> 00:34:28.370
I have a question actually to Ambassador Wittig,
primarily, about 2011 and Syria.

00:34:28.370 --> 00:34:33.110
You were on the Security Council at that point.

00:34:33.110 --> 00:34:35.330
And you lived it very intimately.

00:34:35.330 --> 00:34:41.550
Do you think in hindsight of five years that
something could have been done differently

00:34:41.550 --> 00:34:46.620
in the Council in the first several months
of the Syrian crisis?

00:34:46.620 --> 00:34:52.640
Because the first time the Council was able
to make even a very, very weak statement on

00:34:52.640 --> 00:34:53.910
Syria was August.

00:34:53.910 --> 00:35:00.270
And during those months, things went from
distressing to pretty horrible and then to

00:35:00.270 --> 00:35:02.090
absolutely irreversible.

00:35:02.090 --> 00:35:07.890
WITTIG: What I consider, in hindsight, the
failure of the Security Council to act at

00:35:07.890 --> 00:35:14.190
the beginning of this unfolding disaster in
Syria is probably one of the worst failures

00:35:14.190 --> 00:35:17.010
in the history of the Security Council.

00:35:17.010 --> 00:35:22.420
And I could pinpoint too some factors that
prevented the Security Council to act.

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:30.590
And I think one or two veto powers at the
time were unwilling to sort of jump over their

00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:39.590
shadow and desist from supporting the Assad
regime in 100 percent way.

00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:50.940
And I think that prevented the Council to
open an avenue for a transition regime and

00:35:50.940 --> 00:35:57.800
a dialogue between the various factions in
Syria at an early point, before it morphed

00:35:57.800 --> 00:36:04.270
into that kind of ethnic, religious, tribal
civil war.

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:07.270
So I consider this-and I deplore this.

00:36:07.270 --> 00:36:08.630
I lived through that.

00:36:08.630 --> 00:36:15.260
And I feel this was really a terrible failure
of the Security Council, basically because

00:36:15.260 --> 00:36:20.710
of the veto powers of one or two members.

00:36:20.710 --> 00:36:22.870
ANDERSON: Sir, in the middle.

00:36:22.870 --> 00:36:23.870
Yeah.

00:36:23.870 --> 00:36:24.870
Q: Hi.

00:36:24.870 --> 00:36:27.270
I'm Jim Traub from ForeignPolicy.com.

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:32.230
I wanted to ask about the EU refugee deal
with Turkey, which, Mr. Miliband, you mentioned

00:36:32.230 --> 00:36:33.230
disparagingly.

00:36:33.230 --> 00:36:37.610
And I know that the advocacy community has
been very hostile to it.

00:36:37.610 --> 00:36:42.000
But this is something I think Ambassador Wittig
could address also, because my sense is that

00:36:42.000 --> 00:36:48.450
Chancellor Merkel thought that the only way
that she could gain the confidence of not

00:36:48.450 --> 00:36:53.880
only the German public but European publics
was to convince them that state leaders had

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:59.150
gained control over state borders, and that
this was a recognition of a political necessity,

00:36:59.150 --> 00:37:02.490
which may be an ugly necessity, but it's a
very real one.

00:37:02.490 --> 00:37:08.740
So is your sense that that deal-that political
necessity is not so great, that that deal

00:37:08.740 --> 00:37:12.590
should not have been stuck, that something
altogether different should be done, or rather

00:37:12.590 --> 00:37:17.370
that maybe its terms, its better terms, need
to be more enforced than they have been so

00:37:17.370 --> 00:37:18.370
far?

00:37:18.370 --> 00:37:19.370
MILIBAND: Yeah, no, obviously it's the latter.

00:37:19.370 --> 00:37:24.980
I mean, to be fair to myself, what I said
was that the deal had engineered a pause,

00:37:24.980 --> 00:37:30.270
was it had given the Turkish government a
reason to turn off the tap.

00:37:30.270 --> 00:37:38.130
Now, I believe we are paying the price now
of five, possibly six years in which Europe

00:37:38.130 --> 00:37:40.080
and Turkey have drifted apart.

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:45.800
And there is fault inside Turkey on that,
and there's fault in Europe on that as well.

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:48.520
Actually, it probably started in 2009.

00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:55.040
And the fact that it's now 2016 before Europe
and Turkey are talking about what kind of

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:59.770
deal can address this is probably one indication
that probably Europe is playing catch up.

00:37:59.770 --> 00:38:05.640
To be fair to the prime ministers of Greece
and Italy, in 2013 they were jumping up and

00:38:05.640 --> 00:38:10.350
down saying: Look, this is a crisis that we've
got on our shores here.

00:38:10.350 --> 00:38:15.090
And no one was really listening to them, in
part because you had the euro crisis consuming

00:38:15.090 --> 00:38:19.741
so much political opposition policy, opposition
in Brussels, and also then subsequently because

00:38:19.741 --> 00:38:21.250
of the Ukraine crisis.

00:38:21.250 --> 00:38:28.460
And so the coming refugee crisis in 2013,
2014 didn't really get much attention.

00:38:28.460 --> 00:38:33.400
I think the terms of the deal are problematic
because the one-in/one-out so-called idea,

00:38:33.400 --> 00:38:38.360
if you stop and think about it from a Turkish
point of view, if you want Europe to take

00:38:38.360 --> 00:38:43.610
refugees, the only way you can-(audio break)-then
have them shipped back, and then you replace

00:38:43.610 --> 00:38:45.100
them with someone who goes out.

00:38:45.100 --> 00:38:47.710
And that's what the one-for-one deal means.

00:38:47.710 --> 00:38:52.460
So I think that there's a real problem in
the way that the deal, so-called, has been

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:53.460
structured.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:38:59.440
My own position on this is, one, a legal and
orderly pipeline of people coming from the

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:03.040
Middle East to Europe-not just from Turkey
but from across the Middle East-is essential,

00:39:03.040 --> 00:39:07.400
because if there isn't a route to hope, a
legal route to hope for people, then they

00:39:07.400 --> 00:39:09.470
will be in the hands of the smugglers.

00:39:09.470 --> 00:39:14.320
Secondly, you can only do that when you have
effective European coordination.

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:17.280
I agree with what the ambassador said about
that.

00:39:17.280 --> 00:39:22.700
There's got to be an opt-in from the whole
of the European community.

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:26.210
Thirdly, we haven't talked about this very
much, but European public opinion-there's

00:39:26.210 --> 00:39:30.970
an easy excuse for European public opinion
at the moment when it says, well, what's America

00:39:30.970 --> 00:39:32.310
doing about this?

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:33.570
What's the Gulf doing about this?

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:35.100
There's an easy excuse.

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:37.330
So it's not just Europe that needs to get
its act together.

00:39:37.330 --> 00:39:42.260
There's a global responsibility, because the
truth is that refugee-hosting countries, whether

00:39:42.260 --> 00:39:48.350
it be Turkey, Lebanon, Jordan, or Kenya and
Ethiopia, or Pakistan in the case of Afghans,

00:39:48.350 --> 00:39:52.170
they're delivering on a global public good
and they're not getting supported properly

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:53.170
for it.

00:39:53.170 --> 00:39:57.810
And I think until we address that, we're not
going to be able to get to the roots of the

00:39:57.810 --> 00:39:58.810
problem.

00:39:58.810 --> 00:39:59.810
WITTIG: Can I offer my take on Turkey?

00:39:59.810 --> 00:40:00.810
ANDERSON: Please, please.

00:40:00.810 --> 00:40:01.810
WITTIG: I agree with David.

00:40:01.810 --> 00:40:06.020
I think Europe was ill-prepared for this refugee
crisis.

00:40:06.020 --> 00:40:09.170
We could have seen the writing on the wall.

00:40:09.170 --> 00:40:17.470
And I think the European societies maybe didn't
see it because other problems were so preeminent.

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:27.010
But on Turkey, Mr. Traub, I would say the
leaders, and in particular the chancellor,

00:40:27.010 --> 00:40:34.570
German chancellor, faced a situation where,
in 2015, we had taken in 1.1 million refugees

00:40:34.570 --> 00:40:41.630
alone, so that the equivalent for the U.S.
would be 4.4 million refugees in the year.

00:40:41.630 --> 00:40:47.500
And she knew that this couldn't go on in that
same pace and that same speed.

00:40:47.500 --> 00:40:53.740
And so I think the starting point for this
was to realize that Turkey is the gateway-or

00:40:53.740 --> 00:41:00.180
was the gateway for all the refugees coming
from the Middle East and in particular Syria

00:41:00.180 --> 00:41:01.850
and Iraq.

00:41:01.850 --> 00:41:10.920
So Turkey is and was the key element to get
a grip on a kind of more orderly controlled

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:14.190
migration and refugee flow.

00:41:14.190 --> 00:41:20.610
And we don't see eye to eye on many issues
with the Turkish leadership, or on some issues,

00:41:20.610 --> 00:41:27.550
but in a way that deal has worked in bringing
down the numbers dramatically.

00:41:27.550 --> 00:41:34.850
And so the philosophy of that agreement with
Turkey is take away the incentive for refugees

00:41:34.850 --> 00:41:42.910
to use human traffickers to cross the Aegean
from Turkey to Greece rather than going through

00:41:42.910 --> 00:41:53.530
the procedures in Turkey, and that sort of
taking the human trafficking, which is a multibillion-dollar

00:41:53.530 --> 00:41:59.260
business, out of business, that was the philosophy
of the deal.

00:41:59.260 --> 00:42:08.380
And, you know, I think critics should see
that so far it worked, with all the flaws

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:15.310
that might exist in the procedure, between
Greece and Turkey.

00:42:15.310 --> 00:42:17.070
ANDERSON: OK.

00:42:17.070 --> 00:42:23.400
Right here in the front.

00:42:23.400 --> 00:42:24.400
Q: Thank you.

00:42:24.400 --> 00:42:26.130
Evelyn Leopold, journalist at the United Nations.

00:42:26.130 --> 00:42:28.990
Nice to see you again, Herr Wittig.

00:42:28.990 --> 00:42:30.141
A question for you.

00:42:30.141 --> 00:42:32.530
You mentioned Eastern Europe.

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:40.220
The anti-immigrant movement in Germany is
in Dresden, the former East Germany.

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:48.430
And the further you go east, the more spurs
of racism and unwelcome gestures to immigrants.

00:42:48.430 --> 00:42:55.280
It is because they're not used to having grown
up with any foreigners, or they have a lower

00:42:55.280 --> 00:42:57.050
economic level, or what is it?

00:42:57.050 --> 00:43:03.941
And, Mr. Miliband, while you're asking the
secretary-general a lot of questions, can

00:43:03.941 --> 00:43:08.460
you be British and insist on fluency in the
English language?

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:09.610
(Laughter.)

00:43:09.610 --> 00:43:11.910
MILIBAND: (Laughs.)

00:43:11.910 --> 00:43:19.630
WITTIG: Well, Evelyn, it's an irony-to come
to your question, it's an irony that in many

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:28.050
parts of Europe the hostility, the xenophobic
reactions are most strong in those parts where

00:43:28.050 --> 00:43:30.630
there are no refugees.

00:43:30.630 --> 00:43:35.940
And one of the explanations is that of course
there are many people who are not used to

00:43:35.940 --> 00:43:37.250
change.

00:43:37.250 --> 00:43:44.220
And I would try to understand some of the
Eastern European countries and also the eastern

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:45.220
part of Germany.

00:43:45.220 --> 00:43:54.330
They have gone through so much change in their
life, and not all people of all ages can take

00:43:54.330 --> 00:43:55.940
a lot of change in their lives.

00:43:55.940 --> 00:44:06.860
So I think this would be sort of the benevolent,
benign explanation, that in many cases those

00:44:06.860 --> 00:44:09.960
who turn against refugees are the losers of
globalization.

00:44:09.960 --> 00:44:12.830
And this is sort of a Western phenomenon.

00:44:12.830 --> 00:44:15.400
We see this here in this country as well.

00:44:15.400 --> 00:44:22.050
And this, I think, is an incentive for politicians,
for leaders not to just write those people

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:32.610
off but somehow try not to lose them, explain
to them why it might be beneficial to invest

00:44:32.610 --> 00:44:34.510
in integration, et cetera.

00:44:34.510 --> 00:44:45.870
That's a huge task, I know, but I think it's
important to realize that there were losers.

00:44:45.870 --> 00:44:53.670
The last decades were not just a win-win game
and we've got to look after those losers in

00:44:53.670 --> 00:44:56.420
a more sustainable manner.

00:44:56.420 --> 00:44:57.660
MILIBAND: Can I make a point?

00:44:57.660 --> 00:44:58.660
ANDERSON: Sure.

00:44:58.660 --> 00:45:03.120
MILIBAND: Sorry to prevent a question, but
I want to make a point that links quite a

00:45:03.120 --> 00:45:06.620
lot of the discussion we had and quite a lot
of the questions already.

00:45:06.620 --> 00:45:13.100
Until the eurozone starts growing fast, there's
going to be a-it's going to be next to impossible

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:14.110
to make this work.

00:45:14.110 --> 00:45:22.410
I mean, the economics underpinning integration
are absolutely vital to maintaining consent.

00:45:22.410 --> 00:45:28.740
And there's been-in the first quarter of this
year the figures are better, not 0.6 percent

00:45:28.740 --> 00:45:34.360
growth, but there's never been a better time
for big infrastructure spending, given the

00:45:34.360 --> 00:45:36.670
low interest rates around the world.

00:45:36.670 --> 00:45:40.660
But that means really thinking hard about
some of the economic imbalances within the

00:45:40.660 --> 00:45:43.310
European Union, never mind globally.

00:45:43.310 --> 00:45:46.560
And that obviously means there's big responsibility
in Germany.

00:45:46.560 --> 00:45:52.810
But my honest fear is that unless-until the
economics shifts, we are going to find it

00:45:52.810 --> 00:45:55.810
very, very hard to make this work.

00:45:55.810 --> 00:46:05.320
I mean, the power of economic growth to grease
the wheels of integration and to dilute tension

00:46:05.320 --> 00:46:06.320
cannot be underestimated.

00:46:06.320 --> 00:46:10.620
I mean, in the U.K. you've got a huge level
of concern about immigration in this Brexit

00:46:10.620 --> 00:46:11.620
debate.

00:46:11.620 --> 00:46:12.740
Unemployment is at 5 percent.

00:46:12.740 --> 00:46:17.330
If unemployment were at 10 percent, I guarantee
you it would be a much, much more difficult

00:46:17.330 --> 00:46:18.880
argument to win.

00:46:18.880 --> 00:46:22.860
Thank goodness unemployment in Germany is
relatively low, but there is an economic element

00:46:22.860 --> 00:46:27.850
to the stress that's felt in countries like
France, about which I'm worried, that is really

00:46:27.850 --> 00:46:29.080
fundamental.

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:35.080
ANDERSON: You said earlier that the United
States and the Gulf hadn't stepped up and

00:46:35.080 --> 00:46:40.480
therefore we should before we criticize Europeans
and so forth.

00:46:40.480 --> 00:46:42.820
What would you ask the Gulf to do?

00:46:42.820 --> 00:46:45.410
MILIBAND: Well, they can sign the Refugee
Convention, for starters.

00:46:45.410 --> 00:46:51.130
That would be a good-that would be-
ANDERSON: But would you expect them resettle-to

00:46:51.130 --> 00:46:53.130
have refugees come and-
MILIBAND: Look, one's got to be fair about

00:46:53.130 --> 00:46:56.860
this in the Gulf, because some people say,
just look at the figures for Saudi Arabia

00:46:56.860 --> 00:46:59.600
or for UAE; they have zero refugees.

00:46:59.600 --> 00:47:02.110
Well, that's true because they don't sign
the convention.

00:47:02.110 --> 00:47:07.150
That doesn't mean there are zero Syrians in
Saudi Arabia or zero Syrians in the UAE.

00:47:07.150 --> 00:47:11.170
There are 500,000 Syrians in Saudi Arabia
and 120,000 Syrians in the UAE.

00:47:11.170 --> 00:47:14.430
They're just not classified as refugees.

00:47:14.430 --> 00:47:15.820
Many of them were there before the war.

00:47:15.820 --> 00:47:20.210
I haven't got, off the top of my head, the
figures for who have come in the last five

00:47:20.210 --> 00:47:26.690
years, but I think that we should be upholding
the basic principle that all countries should

00:47:26.690 --> 00:47:31.710
sign the Refugee Convention and that all should
contribute to the delivery of this global

00:47:31.710 --> 00:47:33.490
public good.

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:36.450
ANDERSON: OK.

00:47:36.450 --> 00:47:39.410
Right here.

00:47:39.410 --> 00:47:41.340
Q: Thank you.

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:45.360
Felice Gaer, the Jacob Blaustein Institute.

00:47:45.360 --> 00:47:54.600
You spoke of the practical and economic problems
that are created by the current waves of migration

00:47:54.600 --> 00:47:55.980
and refugees.

00:47:55.980 --> 00:47:59.870
Arthur Helton also cared about the legal aspects.

00:47:59.870 --> 00:48:04.960
And when speaking about the Refugee Convention,
I'm wondering if you think this is a time

00:48:04.960 --> 00:48:09.070
for the definition of "refugee" to be looked
at again.

00:48:09.070 --> 00:48:11.360
Is that an issue?

00:48:11.360 --> 00:48:17.630
And secondly are the people who are raising
security concerns irrelevant?

00:48:17.630 --> 00:48:24.500
Or how would you address those concerns, even
if there are only 40 that you can count who

00:48:24.500 --> 00:48:29.120
are unaccompanied men in the U.S.?
MILIBAND: Well, let me take the second question

00:48:29.120 --> 00:48:30.120
first.

00:48:30.120 --> 00:48:34.750
I have absolutely no hesitation in saying,
as a humanitarian, it's absolutely right to

00:48:34.750 --> 00:48:39.160
have security as a factor that plays into
this.

00:48:39.160 --> 00:48:45.230
But I can also say the U.S., partly because
of the blessings of geography, has an extraordinary

00:48:45.230 --> 00:48:50.540
opportunity to vet those who are trying to-who
are applying to come here and to weed out

00:48:50.540 --> 00:48:51.710
anyone you don't want.

00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:53.220
So it takes 18 to 24 months.

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:55.340
There are 12 to 15 government agencies involved.

00:48:55.340 --> 00:48:56.730
There are biometric testing.

00:48:56.730 --> 00:49:01.120
And if people can't prove who they are, you
don't have to let them in.

00:49:01.120 --> 00:49:06.060
The burden of proof is on the refugee to prove
that they're going to be productive citizens,

00:49:06.060 --> 00:49:08.590
not on you to stop them coming.

00:49:08.590 --> 00:49:09.590
You don't have to take anyone.

00:49:09.590 --> 00:49:11.760
You can-you can make your own choices.

00:49:11.760 --> 00:49:14.170
And that's what makes this particularly distressing.

00:49:14.170 --> 00:49:19.720
It's doubling distressing when you think about
what refugees have contributed to this country,

00:49:19.720 --> 00:49:22.150
because we're talking about a quote, unquote
"burden."

00:49:22.150 --> 00:49:27.540
We're talking about everyone from Albert Einstein,
to Madeline Albright, to Sergey Brin, to Andy

00:49:27.540 --> 00:49:31.770
Grove, who recently, but he was resettled
by the IRC from Hungary in 1956, and he goes

00:49:31.770 --> 00:49:32.770
on to found Intel.

00:49:32.770 --> 00:49:38.040
I mean, this is a country that has not just
done the right thing in admitting refugees,

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:39.560
it's done a smart thing.

00:49:39.560 --> 00:49:44.610
And they actually help produce the country
that we all live in now.

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:50.990
So I think that it's right to address the
security concerns, but it's not right to fall

00:49:50.990 --> 00:49:52.850
for fearmongering.

00:49:52.850 --> 00:49:56.510
And the idea that people are flowing in here
without any checks is just simply untrue.

00:49:56.510 --> 00:49:58.870
And we can speak to that.

00:49:58.870 --> 00:50:01.580
On the legal side, I'll tell you my real fear
there.

00:50:01.580 --> 00:50:08.000
I think once you open up the 1951 convention
and the 1967 amendments, I don't know where

00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:09.910
it will end up.

00:50:09.910 --> 00:50:13.260
And I think the danger is that we lose the
gains that have been made.

00:50:13.260 --> 00:50:14.860
Now, there are real issues about this.

00:50:14.860 --> 00:50:19.540
You know, someone who comes from Syria to
Jordan, who lives in Jordan for two years,

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:24.760
and then moves on, yes, they're a refugee,
but they've actually been living in another

00:50:24.760 --> 00:50:25.760
country.

00:50:25.760 --> 00:50:26.760
They're not a refugee from Jordan.

00:50:26.760 --> 00:50:27.760
They're a refugee from Syria.

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:29.810
So there are-there are issues there.

00:50:29.810 --> 00:50:34.200
A well-founded fear of persecution, how does
that extend to fearing that you're going to

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:36.320
have a barrel bomb dropped on the house that
you're living in?

00:50:36.320 --> 00:50:38.360
I mean, there are issues there.

00:50:38.360 --> 00:50:44.292
But my own political judgement would be to
defend the distinction between the refugee

00:50:44.292 --> 00:50:48.520
and an immigrant-not that one is good and
the other's bad, but they're just different-and

00:50:48.520 --> 00:50:55.410
not to open up the legal questions, because
I think that-I don't think you'll get to a

00:50:55.410 --> 00:51:00.870
resolution, and if you do it won't be a-it
won't be a happy one.

00:51:00.870 --> 00:51:03.200
WITTIG: Can I add something to it?

00:51:03.200 --> 00:51:11.220
I feel that it is a new category of people
coming to Europe in that instance, in a new

00:51:11.220 --> 00:51:12.330
gray zone.

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:18.690
They are neither sort of politically persecuted,
or not fleeing outright civil war.

00:51:18.690 --> 00:51:26.360
So they don't really qualify as a valid asylum
seeker, nor are they are just economic migrants

00:51:26.360 --> 00:51:28.350
that come for a good job.

00:51:28.350 --> 00:51:34.120
But people have lost hope in the future of
their country, that is fragile and unstable,

00:51:34.120 --> 00:51:37.280
and don't see any future for their kids.

00:51:37.280 --> 00:51:40.310
So that's why so many people, for instance,
come from Afghanistan.

00:51:40.310 --> 00:51:45.980
And they are not in one of those two categories,
but in the middle.

00:51:45.980 --> 00:51:49.490
And then the question is, how do we deal with
those people?

00:51:49.490 --> 00:51:52.950
And potentially there are millions more in
those categories.

00:51:52.950 --> 00:51:55.840
ANDERSON: Mmm hmm.

00:51:55.840 --> 00:51:57.770
Yeah, true.

00:51:57.770 --> 00:51:58.770
Sir.

00:51:58.770 --> 00:52:00.510
Q: Jeff Laurenti.

00:52:00.510 --> 00:52:06.880
I wonder if you all might be able to compare
the conditions in which displaced persons

00:52:06.880 --> 00:52:16.760
within Syria, inside Syrian refugee or displaced
person facilities, compare with, A, those

00:52:16.760 --> 00:52:24.390
in the neighboring countries and then, B,
conditions in African situations, because

00:52:24.390 --> 00:52:29.640
they are entirely off our radar screen, yet
as David Miliband told us, you have enormous

00:52:29.640 --> 00:52:34.220
numbers of Africans who are across the border
in refugee camps.

00:52:34.220 --> 00:52:38.650
And one senses that one of the reasons why
when the Italians we're trying to get attention

00:52:38.650 --> 00:52:44.310
was because it wasn't Libyans who were coming,
for all the chaos in Libya across the Mediterranean

00:52:44.310 --> 00:52:46.930
it Italy, it was largely black Africans.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:55.910
Do we have comparable levels of care, of support,
of sustenance in these different situations?

00:52:55.910 --> 00:53:01.770
Or are some more privileged refugees, in a
sense, than others, both in terms of international

00:53:01.770 --> 00:53:06.490
attention and in terms of the resources devoted
to them and willingness to take them in?

00:53:06.490 --> 00:53:09.290
WITTIG: I defer to you.

00:53:09.290 --> 00:53:14.210
MILIBAND: So if you're an internally displaced
person in Syria, that's the worst possible

00:53:14.210 --> 00:53:19.730
situation to be in, not least because there's
still grave danger to your life.

00:53:19.730 --> 00:53:25.820
And the fighting is carrying on and the bombing
raids are carrying on and your life is danger

00:53:25.820 --> 00:53:27.570
and there are not IDP centers.

00:53:27.570 --> 00:53:33.620
You know, we are delivering health care in
the midst of bombed out buildings.

00:53:33.620 --> 00:53:40.930
So then, secondly, the refugees who are in
Turkey, and Jordan, and Lebanon, they're middle

00:53:40.930 --> 00:53:46.590
class, compared to African refugees, partly
because they're middle class people, secondly

00:53:46.590 --> 00:53:51.250
because they've moved into societies that
are more developed societies.

00:53:51.250 --> 00:53:54.750
But that doesn't mean that there aren't hellish
and difficult conditions that people are living

00:53:54.750 --> 00:53:55.750
in.

00:53:55.750 --> 00:53:56.780
The vast bulk of them are not in camps.

00:53:56.780 --> 00:53:58.750
The vast bulk of them are not allowed to work.

00:53:58.750 --> 00:54:04.230
The vast bulk of them are being missed out
by different bits of the humanitarian sector.

00:54:04.230 --> 00:54:08.600
You can go all the way across the Beqaa Valley
and you'll see hundreds of thousands of people

00:54:08.600 --> 00:54:14.050
whose kids are not getting an education, 200,000
kids Syrian kids in Lebanon are not getting

00:54:14.050 --> 00:54:15.050
an education.

00:54:15.050 --> 00:54:17.570
There's no prospect of them having housing.

00:54:17.570 --> 00:54:19.680
There's informal settlements.

00:54:19.680 --> 00:54:23.720
It's a very, very difficult-they're subject
to new visa checks.

00:54:23.720 --> 00:54:24.720
It's a very, very difficult situation.

00:54:24.720 --> 00:54:25.810
They've run out of savings.

00:54:25.810 --> 00:54:27.600
These are the people who came with savings.

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:35.550
I'm glad you mentioned the situation in Africa
because it's not-it's very, very serious to

00:54:35.550 --> 00:54:37.840
be displaced from a poor country to another
poor country.

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:40.790
I mean, that's a very, very tough situation
to be in.

00:54:40.790 --> 00:54:44.990
Although, I would tell you that your survival
chances inside a refugee camp in Kenya are

00:54:44.990 --> 00:54:50.900
greater than those Kenyans who are living
outside the refugee camp.

00:54:50.900 --> 00:54:54.160
And that is a big political problem.

00:54:54.160 --> 00:54:59.390
ANDERSON: I'm afraid this is going to have
to be the last question.

00:54:59.390 --> 00:55:00.390
So please make it short.

00:55:00.390 --> 00:55:02.040
Right here, on the-yes.

00:55:02.040 --> 00:55:05.750
MILIBAND: You should let the people in the
back speak.

00:55:05.750 --> 00:55:07.930
Q: The question I have-oh, Stephen Black (sp),
sorry.

00:55:07.930 --> 00:55:09.370
What happens tomorrow?

00:55:09.370 --> 00:55:14.660
As the discussion went on, the recommendations
offered are wise, often, but they're middle

00:55:14.660 --> 00:55:20.440
term at best, in cases of, say, faster growth
in Europe longer term.

00:55:20.440 --> 00:55:24.715
But the situation in the source countries
of refugees is not going to get better.

00:55:24.715 --> 00:55:26.610
The situation in the camps isn't going to
get better.

00:55:26.610 --> 00:55:30.950
So what happens tomorrow, what happens next
week, what happens in the next year?

00:55:30.950 --> 00:55:32.810
What is this situation going to look like?

00:55:32.810 --> 00:55:39.220
And what can be done, in your view, more immediately
to deal with these concerns, rather than waiting

00:55:39.220 --> 00:55:44.641
for European attitudes to change and European
growth to increase, and so on?

00:55:44.641 --> 00:55:48.870
MILIBAND: So you are going to go out of this
meeting, you're going to visit the IRC website,

00:55:48.870 --> 00:55:49.870
rescue.org.

00:55:49.870 --> 00:55:56.160
You're going to rally your community-(laughter)-to
become part of the IRC family by volunteering

00:55:56.160 --> 00:56:00.740
in the New York or New Jersey resettlement
office to mentor new refugees.

00:56:00.740 --> 00:56:05.240
You're going to rally your local church or
synagogue or community to become donors to

00:56:05.240 --> 00:56:06.830
the IRC, so that we can do more.

00:56:06.830 --> 00:56:12.260
You're going to talk to your plutocratic friends
and make them-(laughter)-signature investors

00:56:12.260 --> 00:56:14.420
in the IRC.

00:56:14.420 --> 00:56:20.730
And I say that not just because it's my job
as the head of an NGO to urge you to support

00:56:20.730 --> 00:56:26.000
us, but because American leadership has got
to mean American leadership.

00:56:26.000 --> 00:56:31.090
And the truth is, that on humanitarian questions
now, Europe is not one country-I mean, Europe's

00:56:31.090 --> 00:56:35.320
28 country-but Europe is now a larger humanitarian
donor than the U.S.

00:56:35.320 --> 00:56:40.650
In a way, it's not fair to compare 28 countries
to one country, but you like to think of yourselves

00:56:40.650 --> 00:56:44.210
as the most generous nation on Earth, as the
people who are at the forefront of all of

00:56:44.210 --> 00:56:46.390
the human progress.

00:56:46.390 --> 00:56:49.060
Here's an opportunity to step forward.

00:56:49.060 --> 00:56:53.700
Secondly, on the refugee resettlement, where
the U.S. has genuinely been a historic leader,

00:56:53.700 --> 00:56:58.330
and has historically taken 45 to 50 percent
of the world's resettled refugee, you're not

00:56:58.330 --> 00:56:59.330
going to be doing that this year.

00:56:59.330 --> 00:57:04.530
I mean, this gentleman's country is going
to be the world leader in refugee resettlement.

00:57:04.530 --> 00:57:11.050
And it is within the power of the executive
branch of your political system not to say

00:57:11.050 --> 00:57:13.190
we'll have 10,000 refugees.

00:57:13.190 --> 00:57:17.100
You can decide how many refugees you take.

00:57:17.100 --> 00:57:21.910
And you can decide what sort of pressure goes
on Congress for overseas aid.

00:57:21.910 --> 00:57:28.310
And you can therefore decide how you're going
to share the burdens of globalization, while

00:57:28.310 --> 00:57:30.690
you enjoy the blessings of globalization.

00:57:30.690 --> 00:57:34.170
And as someone who's not an American, but
does live here and obviously admires the country

00:57:34.170 --> 00:57:39.970
hugely, there's an absolutely fundamental
debate to be had about what it means to be

00:57:39.970 --> 00:57:41.230
the world's global superpower.

00:57:41.230 --> 00:57:42.230
You're not a hyper-power.

00:57:42.230 --> 00:57:44.710
You're not a dictatorial power.

00:57:44.710 --> 00:57:48.160
But you all agree that you're the indispensable
power.

00:57:48.160 --> 00:57:53.620
And figuring out what it means to be an indispensable
power in this humanitarian sector is something

00:57:53.620 --> 00:57:56.700
that needs to-that's something that does need
to be debated and argued over.

00:57:56.700 --> 00:58:02.030
And frankly, policy change tomorrow, the president's
got a summit in the U.N. on the 20th of September.

00:58:02.030 --> 00:58:04.100
What's going to come out of it?

00:58:04.100 --> 00:58:08.450
What new commitments is the U.S. going to
be making at that summit?

00:58:08.450 --> 00:58:14.470
And the fact that Richard told me before the-before
the show-(laughter)-before the meeting started

00:58:14.470 --> 00:58:21.240
that the Council is dedicating itself to global
literacy around the-is that right?

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:25.840
Yeah, global literacy around the U.S., how
American citizens think of themselves not

00:58:25.840 --> 00:58:29.101
just as American citizens but as global citizens,
that's the kind of thing that's really, really

00:58:29.101 --> 00:58:33.340
important because we all know that the lessons
of history are that when countries like the

00:58:33.340 --> 00:58:37.690
U.S. turn inward, there's only trouble around
the corner.

00:58:37.690 --> 00:58:41.790
So I hope there are some really practical
things that you can do coming out of the meeting.

00:58:41.790 --> 00:58:50.040
WITTIG: I cannot-I cannot top that, so I subscribe
to every word.

00:58:50.040 --> 00:58:51.040
(Applause.)

00:58:51.040 --> 00:58:58.890
ANDERSON: On behalf of all of us here, I want
to thank the Council and Arthur Helton's family

00:58:58.890 --> 00:59:02.650
for permitting us to have what was a very
interesting and fruitful discussion.

00:59:02.650 --> 00:59:03.650
And thank you both.

00:59:03.650 --> 00:59:04.650
MILIBND: Thank you very much.

00:59:04.650 --> 00:59:05.650
Thank you, Ambassador.

00:59:05.650 --> 00:59:06.650
(Laughs.)

00:59:06.650 --> 00:59:07.650
(Applause.)

00:59:07.650 --> 00:59:07.651
(END)

